            <ArticleTitle>Effect of left ventricular systolic dysfunction on secondary medical prevention and clinical outcome in stable coronary artery disease patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>35-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1875-2136(16)30113-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.acvd.2016.04.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Limited recent data are available in the literature on whether the presence of left ventricular systolic dysfunction (LVSD) affects the therapeutic management of patients with stable coronary artery disease (CAD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The objectives of this study were to analyse prevalence, effect on therapeutics and prognosis of LVSD in stable CAD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We prospectively included 4184 CAD outpatients free from any myocardial infarction or coronary revascularization for&gt;1year. Left ventricular ejection fraction (EF) was available for 4124 (98.6%) patients. Follow-up was performed at 2years. All events were adjudicated blindly.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean EF was 57.5±10.8%, and 201 (4.9%) patients had an EF≤35%. The prescription of renin-angiotensin system inhibitors and beta-blockers was inversely related to EF, and reached&gt;90% in patients with EF≤35%. Seventy-five (37.3%) of the patients with EF≤35% received a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist. Eighty-five (42.3%) of the patients with EF≤35% had an implantable cardioverter defibrillator. Clinical follow-up data were obtained for 4090 patients (99.2%). Event rates were higher in patients with low EF (adjusted hazard ratio [95% confidence interval] for EF≤35%, with EF≥60% as reference: 3.93 [2.60-5.93] and 7.12 [3.85-13.18], for all-cause death and cardiovascular death, respectively).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In patients with stable CAD, LVSD is well taken into account by cardiologists, with extensive use of evidence-based medications and interventions. Despite this, LVSD remains a major prognostic indicator in this population.</AbstractText>
